WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:00.953
- Welcome back.

00:00:00.953 --> 00:00:02.160
We're here with Elisabeth Moss, y'all!

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:03.107
Give it up.

00:00:03.107 --> 00:00:05.040
(crowd cheering and clapping)

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:07.350
Our next guest is a very talented musician

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:09.180
from a musically-gifted family.

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:11.490
Her debut album is called
"The Hardest Part,"

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:13.507
and off that album, her new single is

00:00:13.507 --> 00:00:15.390
"Noah (Stand Still)".

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:16.560
Cool title.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:18.870
You can find both wherever
you get your music,

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:21.840
and you can find her in
the back of the most fun

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:23.193
Uber ride ever.

00:00:23.193 --> 00:00:25.776
(upbeat music)

00:00:28.482 --> 00:00:31.315
(music continues)

00:00:34.982 --> 00:00:37.815
(music continues)

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:40.380
Well,

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:42.580
I'm going to have to know
how that night ended.

00:00:42.580 --> 00:00:43.413
(Elisabeth laughs)

00:00:43.413 --> 00:00:45.030
And I'm disappointed in
every Uber driver I've

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:45.930
ever had at this point.

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:47.818
Say hello to Noah Cyrus!

00:00:47.818 --> 00:00:49.882
(audience cheering)

00:00:49.882 --> 00:00:53.049
(jazzy music playing)

00:00:55.779 --> 00:00:59.612
(cheering and music continue)

00:01:04.799 --> 00:01:08.632
(cheering and music continue)

00:01:09.934 --> 00:01:11.434
[Kelly] I love it!

00:01:13.144 --> 00:01:14.580
Well, so have y'all ever met before?

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:15.600
- No! We have not. - No.

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:16.433
- [Kelly] Y'all never met?

00:01:16.433 --> 00:01:17.537
- [Noah] It's so nice to meet you!

00:01:17.537 --> 00:01:18.370
- I'm a very big fan.

00:01:18.370 --> 00:01:19.203
- [Kelly] Oh my god.

00:01:19.203 --> 00:01:20.049
I know. This rock and roll
vibe you've got going on.

00:01:20.049 --> 00:01:20.957
(singing) Hey.

00:01:20.957 --> 00:01:21.790
(Normal voice) I like it.

00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:23.320
So, real quick, Noah

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:26.790
What company? How do you
get that Uber driver?

00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:27.623
- [Noah] Oh, my.

00:01:27.623 --> 00:01:28.680
- Like, that ... What happened there?

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:30.420
- Okay, so

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:32.490
my friend actually, like,

00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:35.148
knew Vic before I did. I was like ...

00:01:35.148 --> 00:01:35.981
- [Kelly] Vic.

00:01:35.981 --> 00:01:38.460
- I would see Vic on my
friend's Instagram stories,

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:41.724
and I would be like, "This looks insane."

00:01:41.724 --> 00:01:42.557
- [Kelly] Fun.

00:01:42.557 --> 00:01:43.500
- Insane! Insane.

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:44.333
- [Kelly] Yes.

00:01:44.333 --> 00:01:47.070
- And I was in need of a ride one night,

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:49.770
and Vic, Vic was there for me,

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:51.323
and it was ...

00:01:51.323 --> 00:01:52.156
- [Kelly] With a party!

00:01:52.156 --> 00:01:54.690
- [Noah] It was, it
was pretty nuts. Um ...

00:01:54.690 --> 00:01:55.523
- [Kelly] It's crazy!

00:01:55.523 --> 00:01:57.060
- [Noah] You know, at
the end of this video,

00:01:57.060 --> 00:01:58.080
I don't think you can hear it,

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:00.492
but he says, "Get on my shoulders."

00:02:00.492 --> 00:02:01.325
(Noah laughs)

00:02:01.325 --> 00:02:02.158
- [Kelly] You're like,
"You know what ... "

00:02:02.158 --> 00:02:04.327
- [Noah] And we're in the
McDonald's drive-through.

00:02:04.327 --> 00:02:05.220
(Kelly laughs)

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:06.053
- [Kelly] What?!

00:02:06.053 --> 00:02:08.160
- [Noah] Yeah. So that ... Yeah.

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:10.459
- Someone should tell Vic
you can't say that anymore.

00:02:10.459 --> 00:02:11.430
(Kelly laughs)

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:12.270
That's amazing!

00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:13.260
- Yeah. Yeah. We were ...

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:15.630
- [Kelly] He looks like a fun experience.

00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:16.800
I feel like that should be, like, a thing

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:17.760
when people come to L.A.

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:18.794
- [Noah] It's an experience.

00:02:18.794 --> 00:02:20.220
- Like, "Oh, you've got
to take a ride with Vic."

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:21.053
Yeah.

00:02:21.053 --> 00:02:22.140
- Maybe not the shoulders. But yes.

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:23.097
- [Noah] It's great.

00:02:23.097 --> 00:02:23.930
(Elisabeth and Kelly laughing)

00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:25.890
So wait. So your debut album
is called "The Hardest Part"

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:28.950
which is such a cool title, by the way,

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:30.060
just from musician to musician.

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:32.400
I just love how you're
like, "What is this about?

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:33.930
This could be about so many things."

00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:36.420
So why did you want to call it that?

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:38.820
- [Noah] Well, it is about so many things.

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:42.627
It was kind of the hardest
part of a lot of things.

00:02:42.627 --> 00:02:43.460
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:44.790
- Um, "The Hardest
Part" is the title track

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:46.560
of the record, as well.

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:48.000
Um.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:51.510
Which kind of touches on
the subject of going home

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:55.410
and getting older and kind of

00:02:55.410 --> 00:02:57.870
finding yourself

00:02:57.870 --> 00:02:58.703
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:58.703 --> 00:02:59.536
- [Noah] As an adult.

00:02:59.536 --> 00:03:03.480
And also touched on, um ... I'm about ...

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:05.940
Um, in December will be
two years into recovery

00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:08.585
of off Xanax, uh, addiction, and so I ...

00:03:08.585 --> 00:03:09.630
- [Kelly] Oh, wow! I didn't know that!

00:03:09.630 --> 00:03:10.463
- [Noah] Okay. Yeah!

00:03:10.463 --> 00:03:11.859
So I, um ...

00:03:11.859 --> 00:03:12.692
- No.

00:03:12.692 --> 00:03:13.525
- [Noah] I touch on that
in the album, and, um ...

00:03:13.525 --> 00:03:15.810
- [Kelly] Hey, that's so
important that you're talking

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:19.080
about that because a lot
of people are addicted

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:20.400
to, like, pharmaceuticals in general.

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:21.780
Like, that's an important
thing that people

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:22.830
do not talk about.

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:25.050
- It's ... it was

00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:26.910
an extremely

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:27.840
challenging

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:28.740
couple of years.

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:29.573
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:03:29.573 --> 00:03:31.740
- And, um, I think, in the pandemic,

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:32.850
a lot of us can agree.

00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:35.917
We all were forced into isolation

00:03:35.917 --> 00:03:37.140
and um

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:40.740
just really confronting and dealing with

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:43.830
all of our anxieties, and our depression,

00:03:43.830 --> 00:03:48.300
and mental health, and when
addiction was in the picture

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:52.110
with me, I just furthered
my self into that.

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:53.165
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:03:53.165 --> 00:03:57.390
- And I was also really in
love at the time of the, um,

00:03:57.390 --> 00:04:01.680
pandemic, and I think I was
just as addicted to that person

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:03.780
as I was the substances,

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:05.970
and when substance abuse, I think,

00:04:05.970 --> 00:04:07.380
is in the mix of a relationship,

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:10.440
it creates a pretty toxic environment.

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:11.490
- [Kelly] Yes. Yeah.

00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:13.950
- [Noah] And so I touch on
a lot of the hardest part

00:04:13.950 --> 00:04:15.420
of a lot of things on this album.

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:19.770
This album, without a doubt,
saved my life, absolutely.

00:04:19.770 --> 00:04:22.980
It gave me so much structure and um ...

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:24.120
- And a vessel, right? And an outlet.

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:25.950
- [Noah] Yeah. That's exactly
what I was going to say.

00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:29.370
An emotional outlet at a
time I really needed it.

00:04:29.370 --> 00:04:30.552
- Yeah.

00:04:30.552 --> 00:04:32.730
- [Noah] Um, and I mean,
I think everyone agrees

00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:35.880
how healing music is.

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:36.713
- [Kelly] Oh, my gosh.

00:04:36.713 --> 00:04:38.730
- And as a musician and I
think even just as listeners

00:04:38.730 --> 00:04:39.936
and, um,

00:04:39.936 --> 00:04:43.590
music is just healing and
can offer you a friend.

00:04:43.590 --> 00:04:44.520
- [Kelly] Such a great connector.

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:45.353
- [Noah] Yeah. Yeah!

00:04:45.353 --> 00:04:46.186
- To not feel alone.

00:04:46.186 --> 00:04:47.070
- That's the greatest gift ever

00:04:47.070 --> 00:04:50.017
when you put a song on
because you're like,

00:04:50.017 --> 00:04:53.010
"Look, I just need to,
like, have a minute."

00:04:53.010 --> 00:04:55.200
And you have, like, a
song or whatever you go.

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:57.610
Do you have a song that you
go to when you're like ...

00:04:57.610 --> 00:04:58.860
- Oh, gosh.

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:00.255
- I go to pretty much
anything Annie Lennox.

00:05:00.255 --> 00:05:01.560
- There's so many. There's so many.

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:03.450
I mean, for me, it's
just that expression that

00:05:03.450 --> 00:05:05.160
sometimes I can't put into words.

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:05.993
You know?

00:05:05.993 --> 00:05:07.890
I can't do what you do. Or you do.

00:05:07.890 --> 00:05:08.948
- Well, I can't do what you do, so ...

00:05:08.948 --> 00:05:10.042
- [Kelly] Yeah. Me neither.

00:05:10.042 --> 00:05:10.875
- And I can't do what you do.

00:05:10.875 --> 00:05:11.708
- [Kelly] Me neither.
I can't do that either.

00:05:11.708 --> 00:05:13.650
- [Elisabeth] I can't write
like that or sing like that,

00:05:13.650 --> 00:05:15.720
so for me, it's sometimes
if you can't put something

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:18.090
into words or express
something, the perfect song

00:05:18.090 --> 00:05:19.980
that one of you has done and written

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:22.860
can really express something
for me, and obviously

00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:25.380
for so many people around the world.

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:28.440
- Yeah. I have, like,
artists more so I go to

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:29.940
that I'm like, "Oh, I need this."

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:31.119
- [Elisabeth] Oh, yeah.

00:05:31.119 --> 00:05:31.952
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:05:31.952 --> 00:05:33.840
- [Kelly] Um, I love this,
too, that your latest single,

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:35.170
it's with your dad, right?

00:05:35.170 --> 00:05:36.003
- [Noah] It is. Yeah.

00:05:36.003 --> 00:05:36.870
- [Kelly] And it's "Noah (Stand Still)"?

00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:37.703
- [Noah] It is.

00:05:37.703 --> 00:05:38.536
- [Kelly] So where does that come from?

00:05:38.536 --> 00:05:40.410
Is that from him telling you to be still?

00:05:40.410 --> 00:05:43.140
- Yeah. So I have "stand
still" here on my arm,

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:43.973
and so does my dad.

00:05:43.973 --> 00:05:45.810
I'm actually covering my pinkie because

00:05:45.810 --> 00:05:49.925
I have all of these
nails but one, um, so ...

00:05:49.925 --> 00:05:51.573
- [Kelly] I've done that
on this show! (laughs)

00:05:51.573 --> 00:05:53.007
- [Noah] I'm just going like this

00:05:53.007 --> 00:05:55.203
for the remainder of our ...

00:05:55.203 --> 00:05:57.390
- I've totally done that
with my ring finger one time,

00:05:57.390 --> 00:05:58.770
and I was like, "I'm going
to look like I'm flashing

00:05:58.770 --> 00:06:00.210
gang signs." I'm like ...

00:06:00.210 --> 00:06:01.710
Because I was trying to hide it.

00:06:01.710 --> 00:06:02.880
I didn't even notice!

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:03.713
You're so good.

00:06:03.713 --> 00:06:05.130
I was looking at your
cool tats, so you're good.

00:06:05.130 --> 00:06:05.963
- [Noah] Oh, thank you.

00:06:05.963 --> 00:06:07.710
So, no. "Stand still" has just been

00:06:07.710 --> 00:06:11.220
around through generations
of my dad's side of my ...

00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:13.290
His grandfather, his dad.

00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:14.123
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:06:14.123 --> 00:06:15.300
- Um, passed down to him,

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:18.270
and has kind of shared that advice of

00:06:18.270 --> 00:06:21.090
when you don't know what
to do, just stand still.

00:06:21.090 --> 00:06:24.240
I took a trip to see my dad,

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:25.073
um,

00:06:25.073 --> 00:06:26.520
I think around August 2020,

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:29.640
which was couple months before I really

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:32.100
committed to recovery.

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:33.480
Um.

00:06:33.480 --> 00:06:36.510
And we stood up on his hill in Nashville,

00:06:36.510 --> 00:06:38.790
and Nashville's just
such a peaceful place.

00:06:38.790 --> 00:06:39.720
- Beautiful, too. Yeah.

00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:41.730
- [Noah] And he had me take my shoes off

00:06:41.730 --> 00:06:43.230
and put my feet in the grass

00:06:43.230 --> 00:06:44.940
and really get connected to home,

00:06:44.940 --> 00:06:46.710
and he said, "Noah, just stand still.

00:06:46.710 --> 00:06:47.700
Like, breathe in the air.

00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:49.950
Just stand still, and
get connected back to

00:06:49.950 --> 00:06:52.290
who you really are. You know?

00:06:52.290 --> 00:06:53.970
None of the rest of this matters.

00:06:53.970 --> 00:06:55.950
Like, let's just be here, me and you."

00:06:55.950 --> 00:06:57.641
And we had such an amazing moment.

00:06:57.641 --> 00:06:58.474
- Oh, wow.

00:06:58.474 --> 00:07:00.810
- [Noah] that I hadn't
had in such a long time,

00:07:00.810 --> 00:07:04.530
and he has no idea.
Well, I mean, he will now

00:07:04.530 --> 00:07:05.580
because he's watching this at home.

00:07:05.580 --> 00:07:06.419
Hi dad!

00:07:06.419 --> 00:07:08.137
(Kelly laughs)

00:07:08.137 --> 00:07:12.353
Um, but he really had no
idea, I think, at the time.

00:07:12.353 --> 00:07:13.186
- [Kelly] The impact.

00:07:13.186 --> 00:07:14.520
- As a parent, as parents, you can see

00:07:14.520 --> 00:07:15.813
your child is struggling,

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:19.470
but he didn't know,
really, how much I really

00:07:19.470 --> 00:07:22.140
needed to hear that be
reminded of that at that time.

00:07:22.140 --> 00:07:24.510
- That's so cool that you're
able to like, have that

00:07:24.510 --> 00:07:25.343
in your family.

00:07:25.343 --> 00:07:26.310
Y'all are musical, and you have that gift

00:07:26.310 --> 00:07:27.180
to be able to share together.

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:28.050
That's really special.

00:07:28.050 --> 00:07:31.650
I challenge everyone to find
something, um, that's just

00:07:31.650 --> 00:07:34.350
artistic because it comes
out in a different way,

00:07:34.350 --> 00:07:36.123
and it's a good release, I think.

